WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.377
AI Weekly, week 45.

00:00:01.377 --> 00:00:04.782
The weekly briefing on artificial intelligence.

00:00:04.782 --> 00:00:09.853
Created automatically with AI, not read by a human before publication.

00:00:09.853 --> 00:00:12.679
Please report errors to ai@bks-lab.com.

00:00:14.079 --> 00:00:15.120
The key points.

00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:24.559
Market crash: The US semiconductor index loses over 12 percent in a single day, and Anthropic now aims for its IPO at a lower valuation.

00:00:24.559 --> 00:00:33.998
Windows 11: A year after end of support, Windows 10 still runs on 27.7 percent of Windows PCs, and expensive memory slows the migration.

00:00:33.998 --> 00:00:41.494
Claude reports: Anthropic gives admins up to ten usage reports per month, and the feature is off by default.

00:00:41.494 --> 00:00:49.545
Open models: DeepSeek simulates attacks in test networks for 2 to 3 dollars, while GPT-6-Sol costs 17 to 46 dollars.

00:00:50.945 --> 00:00:52.392
Our story of the week.

00:00:52.392 --> 00:00:57.122
AI market crash after US midterms: Anthropic lowers expectations for its IPO.

00:00:57.122 --> 00:01:05.169
The PHLX Semiconductor Sector Index lost over 12 percent on Thursday, the Nasdaq fell 5.5 percent and the S&P 500 fell 4.4 percent.

00:01:05.169 --> 00:01:06.644
Nvidia lost 9.3 percent.

00:01:06.644 --> 00:01:12.111
According to Reuters, the Nasdaq 100 lost around 2.5 trillion US dollars in market value.

00:01:12.111 --> 00:01:14.691
The Nasdaq 100 recovered partly on Friday.

00:01:14.691 --> 00:01:16.657
Chart: Price losses on Thursday.

00:01:16.657 --> 00:01:27.837
According to heise, the US midterm elections were the trigger: in dozens of local votes, citizens voted against building AI data centers, with one exception in Harwood, North Dakota.

00:01:27.837 --> 00:01:37.297
The Decoder, by contrast, names rising financing costs and high AI spending plans as the cause, and says the election result only accelerated the selloff.

00:01:37.297 --> 00:01:43.993
According to Bloomberg, as relayed by heise, the crash stems from fully developed fears around the AI sector.

00:01:43.993 --> 00:01:47.249
Anthropic is sticking to its IPO before Thanksgiving.

00:01:47.249 --> 00:01:56.709
According to investors cited by the Financial Times, the company wants to raise 80 to 100 billion dollars at a valuation of 1.25 to 1.75 trillion dollars.

00:01:56.709 --> 00:01:59.535
That is above the current 965 billion dollars.

00:01:59.535 --> 00:02:04.817
According to The Decoder, the reported annualized revenue is about 81 billion dollars.

00:02:04.817 --> 00:02:11.206
According to the Wall Street Journal, one key investor now considers only around one trillion realistic.

00:02:11.206 --> 00:02:17.411
Critical voices: Volume and valuation range rest on investor statements cited by the Financial Times.

00:02:17.411 --> 00:02:25.949
The loss figures also refer to different indices: heise gives 5.5 percent for the Nasdaq, The Decoder gives 6.4 percent for the Nasdaq 100.

00:02:25.949 --> 00:02:27.731
What it means for you: Watch.

00:02:27.731 --> 00:02:37.068
Anyone locking in AI services from large providers for several years should review contract terms, price adjustment clauses and exit options at renewal.

00:02:38.468 --> 00:02:43.558
Next: Windows 11 migration stalls over "chipflation" and "RAMageddon".

00:02:43.558 --> 00:02:48.778
In September, Windows 10 still ran on 27.7 percent of Windows PCs worldwide.

00:02:48.778 --> 00:02:56.197
According to CFO Amy Hood, Microsoft's Windows revenue from PC makers fell 27 percent in the latest quarter.

00:02:56.197 --> 00:03:04.851
PC shipments dropped 21 percent in the third quarter, driven by high memory prices, uncertainty over tariffs and the Iran war.

00:03:04.851 --> 00:03:10.758
For the fourth quarter of 2026, one analyst expects a 27 percent decline year on year.

00:03:10.758 --> 00:03:22.023
Dean McCarron, principal analyst at Mercury Research, describes the mechanism as follows: when prices rise, demand falls, and the replacement cycle shifts backward.

00:03:22.023 --> 00:03:27.518
According to Hyperframe Research, memory supply shortages could last until 2029.

00:03:27.518 --> 00:03:37.959
The 27.7 percent share comes from StatCounter measurement data, and only the fourth-quarter forecast and the shortage expectation are analyst estimates.

00:03:37.959 --> 00:03:40.225
What it means for you: Check now.

00:03:40.225 --> 00:03:50.185
IT leads should secure hardware orders for the Windows 11 migration with price quotes now and list the Windows 10 devices that are still running.

00:03:50.185 --> 00:03:55.299
Next: Smart Reports: Anthropic gives admins insight into Claude usage.

00:03:55.299 --> 00:04:00.034
With "Smart Reports", authorized managers can have Claude usage in the company analyzed.

00:04:00.034 --> 00:04:08.211
According to the company, the feature is free for Enterprise customers but disabled by default, and each company can create up to ten reports per month.

00:04:08.211 --> 00:04:20.694
In a help page article, Anthropic describes the analysis this way: Claude reads a sample of the transcripts in that scope, groups them by workflow and output type, assigns the matching costs to each group and summarizes the results.

00:04:20.694 --> 00:04:28.925
The background is cost concern: according to a Bitkom survey, a third of companies in Germany said they were surprised by the high costs of their AI use.

00:04:28.925 --> 00:04:30.486
What it means for you: Watch.

00:04:30.486 --> 00:04:37.964
Anyone using Claude Enterprise should clarify with data protection and the works council which analyses are permitted before activating it.

00:04:37.964 --> 00:04:44.376
Next: Study: Water demand of German data centers could more than double by 2030.

00:04:44.376 --> 00:04:52.833
German data centers needed around 13 billion liters of water for cooling in 2025, and by 2030 demand could grow to up to 27 billion liters per year.

00:04:52.833 --> 00:04:57.576
This is the result of a short study by the Öko-Institut commissioned by Greenpeace.

00:04:57.576 --> 00:05:02.547
The weighted average is 0.67 liters of water per kilowatt hour of IT power consumption.

00:05:02.547 --> 00:05:10.490
The study warns that new mega data centers could cause acute groundwater stress and distribution conflicts in regions that are already dry.

00:05:10.490 --> 00:05:19.233
It contrasts sharply with the digital industry's account: the industry association Bitkom had shortly before rated water demand as largely unproblematic.

00:05:19.233 --> 00:05:20.890
What it means for you: Watch.

00:05:20.890 --> 00:05:28.604
Anyone tendering colocation or cloud contracts or compiling sustainability reports should ask providers for water consumption per site.

00:05:30.004 --> 00:05:30.947
In brief.

00:05:30.947 --> 00:05:41.297
Briefly noted: TikTok: The EU Commission has preliminarily found the "For You" feed in breach of the Digital Services Act, and this is not a final decision.

00:05:41.297 --> 00:05:51.713
OpenAI: According to a Bloomberg report, OpenAI has so far raised 11 billion dollars at a valuation of 1.25 trillion dollars, against a target of 30 billion.

00:05:51.713 --> 00:05:58.149
Anthropic: According to The Decoder, the reported annualized revenue is about 81 billion dollars.

00:05:58.149 --> 00:06:05.911
Data centers: According to Greenpeace, only 139 reports from around 1,200 operators subject to reporting were usable.

00:06:07.311 --> 00:06:08.438
Tool of the week.

00:06:08.438 --> 00:06:11.240
Perplexity Teammates: Always-on AI agents.

00:06:11.240 --> 00:06:17.779
According to the report, each Teammate runs on its own virtual machine with a desktop and browser.

00:06:17.779 --> 00:06:23.250
Progress is tracked through threads in the app, and instructions go through Slack.

00:06:23.250 --> 00:06:31.657
It requires the Max plan at 200 dollars per month, while competing products start at 100 dollars (Dots) and 20 dollars (Duet).

00:06:31.657 --> 00:06:36.994
In practice, the cheaper offerings are likely to hit their usage limits quickly.

00:06:36.994 --> 00:06:38.462
Report at The Decoder.

00:06:39.862 --> 00:06:40.970
Fail of the week.

00:06:40.970 --> 00:06:47.544
"Meta pauses controversial commercial terms" Meta has paused an update to its commercial terms of use indefinitely.

00:06:47.544 --> 00:06:52.689
It was originally due to take effect on October 30, and the date was moved to November 20.

00:06:52.689 --> 00:06:59.949
Courts in Brazil and Norway issued preliminary injunctions, and the UK competition authority CMA opened a formal investigation.

00:06:59.949 --> 00:07:04.123
A Meta spokesperson told Reuters that the existing terms remain in force.

00:07:04.123 --> 00:07:09.039
Meta stresses that it never intended to use the terms of use to get around robots.txt.

00:07:09.039 --> 00:07:13.898
Cause: The change met judicial and regulatory resistance before it could take effect.

00:07:13.898 --> 00:07:22.130
What we learn: Anyone changing vendor terms on data use and AI training must expect pushback, and customers should actively read change notices.

00:07:23.530 --> 00:07:24.718
Number of the week.

00:07:24.718 --> 00:07:32.656
2 to 3 USD per attack: Token cost of a simulated attack with DeepSeek according to Vals measurements (GPT-6-Sol: 17 to 46 USD).

00:07:32.656 --> 00:07:41.407
The Vals platform measured the token cost of an attack in simulated networks: 2 to 3 dollars with DeepSeek, 17 to 46 dollars with GPT-6-Sol.

00:07:41.407 --> 00:07:50.033
The values concern different models, and according to the source, open models are generally cheaper because they run on your own hardware.

00:07:50.033 --> 00:07:56.908
DeepSeek met 73 percent of the objectives on average, and OpenAI's Daybreak completed 81 percent of the tasks.

00:07:56.908 --> 00:08:09.097
Nikil Ravi, a researcher at Vals, says the best models, which sit in the labs and are available only through access programs, are now being caught up with by open models that anyone can download.

00:08:09.097 --> 00:08:16.723
These are measurements in simulations, not real attacks, and Wired points out that real attackers mostly use older models.

00:08:18.123 --> 00:08:20.222
The reading list for the weekend.

00:08:20.222 --> 00:08:31.224
For the weekend: Studie warnt vor Wasserstress durch neue Mega-Rechenzentren in Deutschland: Compact summary of the Öko-Institut study with Bitkom's counterposition (3 min).

00:08:31.224 --> 00:08:40.254
Free Chinese AI Models Are Getting Scary Good at Hacking: Technically deeper look at the Vals measurement and the cost of open models (5 min).

00:08:40.254 --> 00:08:48.331
Windows-11-Umstieg stockt wegen „Chipflation“ und „RAMageddon“: Data on hardware prices, shipments and Windows revenue (6 min).

00:08:49.731 --> 00:08:50.507
The outlook.

00:08:50.507 --> 00:08:59.881
According to investors cited by the Financial Times, Anthropic wants to complete its IPO before Thanksgiving and raise 80 to 100 billion dollars.

00:08:59.881 --> 00:09:08.027
According to Bloomberg, citing a source, the OpenAI funding round is not yet closed and could reach around 20 billion dollars.

00:09:08.027 --> 00:09:14.363
For TikTok, the EU Commission's final decision is still pending after the preliminary DSA finding.

00:09:15.763 --> 00:09:17.638
That was AI Weekly for week 45.

00:09:17.638 --> 00:09:19.997
All sources are in the written edition.

00:09:19.997 --> 00:09:20.965
Until next week.

